<p class="gna-lede">There are two main surgical ways to make a chin more prominent: placing an implant over the chin bone, or a sliding genioplasty, in which the lower part of the chin bone is cut, moved and fixed in a new position. An implant suits a mild, mainly forward deficiency in a chin of normal height, while a genioplasty can also lengthen, shorten, narrow or straighten the chin. If the real problem is a receding lower jaw and a bite that does not meet properly, neither may be the right answer.</p>

<h2 id="what-is-chin-augmentation">What does chin augmentation involve?</h2>
<p>Chin augmentation means increasing the size or projection of the chin to improve the balance between the nose, lips, chin and neck. It is one of the more common facial procedures because the chin has a strong effect on how the whole profile looks. A chin that sits too far back can make the nose look larger, the lower face look short and the neck line less defined.</p>
<p>The options range from temporary injectable fillers to surgery on the chin itself, and, when the bite is involved, to corrective jaw surgery. Choosing between them starts with an accurate diagnosis rather than with a preferred technique.</p>
<div class="gna-keypoints">
  <h3>Key points</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>A chin implant adds forward projection and some width, and is the simpler of the two operations.</li>
    <li>A sliding genioplasty moves your own bone and can correct projection, height, width and asymmetry.</li>
    <li>Neither operation corrects a bite problem; a receding lower jaw with a large overbite may need jaw surgery instead.</li>
    <li>Fillers can preview or make small changes, but the effect is temporary.</li>
    <li>Recovery, results and risks vary between individuals; temporary lip numbness is common after either surgery.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2 id="chin-or-jaw">Is it your chin or your jaw?</h2>
<p>A chin can look weak for several different reasons, and each is treated differently:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Microgenia</strong>: the chin bone itself is small, while the jaws and bite are normal.</li>
  <li><strong>Retrogenia</strong>: the chin sits back relative to the rest of the jaw.</li>
  <li><strong>Retrognathia</strong>: the whole lower jaw sits back, usually with a large overbite where the upper front teeth stick out well in front of the lower ones.</li>
  <li><strong>A vertical problem</strong>: the chin is too short or too long in height, which changes how prominent it appears.</li>
  <li><strong>Asymmetry</strong>: the chin is shifted or tilted to one side. Our guide to the <a href="/blogs/causes-of-facial-asymmetry-common-medical-dental-and-structural-reasons/">causes of facial asymmetry</a> explains why this happens.</li>
</ul>
<p>To tell these apart, the surgeon examines your face at rest and smiling, checks your bite, and usually takes a side skull X-ray (cephalogram) and sometimes a cone-beam CT scan. Standardised photographs are used for planning and, for more complex cases, <a href="/blogs/role-of-3d-virtual-planning/">3D virtual surgical planning</a> can model the movement in advance.</p>

<h2 id="chin-implant">Chin implant: how it works</h2>
<p>A chin implant is a shaped piece of medical-grade material placed in a pocket directly on the front of the chin bone. The two most widely used materials are:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Solid silicone</strong>, which is smooth and flexible. The body forms a thin capsule of scar tissue around it, which makes it relatively easy to remove or replace.</li>
  <li><strong>Porous high-density polyethylene</strong>, which allows surrounding tissue to grow into it. This helps it stay in place but makes it harder to shape and to remove.</li>
</ul>
<p>The implant is inserted through a short incision in the crease under the chin or through an incision inside the lower lip. It may be fixed to the bone with a small screw to stop it moving. The operation is usually shorter than a genioplasty and can be done under general anaesthesia or, in selected cases, local anaesthesia with sedation.</p>
<p>Implants work well for adding forward projection and a little width. They are less suitable when the chin is too short in height, when the problem is a receding lower jaw caused by a bite problem, or when the chin is significantly asymmetrical. Because an implant is a foreign material, there is a lifelong small risk of infection or displacement, and over time some implants press into the underlying bone.</p>

<h2 id="sliding-genioplasty">Sliding genioplasty: how it works</h2>
<p>A sliding (osseous) genioplasty reshapes the chin using your own bone. Through an incision inside the lower lip, the surgeon makes a horizontal cut in the chin bone below the roots of the teeth and below the nerves that supply feeling to the lip. The freed segment of bone is then moved into its planned position and fixed with a small titanium plate and screws. The surgery is done under general anaesthesia and is often combined with corrective jaw surgery when both are needed.</p>
<p>Because the bone itself is repositioned, a genioplasty can:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Move the chin forward, or back if it is too prominent</li>
  <li>Shorten a long chin by removing a wedge of bone, or lengthen a short one</li>
  <li>Shift the chin sideways or rotate it to improve asymmetry</li>
  <li>Narrow a wide chin or slightly widen a narrow one</li>
</ul>
<p>Moving the bone also moves the muscles and soft tissue attached to it, which tends to give a natural contour. The trade-offs are a more involved operation, more swelling, and a higher chance of temporary lip and chin numbness than with an implant.</p>

<h2 id="comparison">Implant vs genioplasty vs fillers</h2>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>&nbsp;</th><th>Chin implant</th><th>Sliding genioplasty</th><th>Dermal fillers</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>What it does</td><td>Adds material over the front of the bone</td><td>Moves your own chin bone</td><td>Injects gel under the skin</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Can correct</td><td>Forward projection, some width</td><td>Projection, height, width, asymmetry</td><td>Small increases in projection</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Anaesthesia</td><td>General, or local with sedation</td><td>General</td><td>Local numbing cream or injection</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Incision</td><td>Under the chin or inside the lip</td><td>Inside the lip</td><td>None</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Typical early recovery</td><td>About one to two weeks</td><td>About two to three weeks</td><td>A few days of possible swelling or bruising</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Main risks</td><td>Infection, displacement, asymmetry, bone erosion</td><td>Lip numbness, swelling, palpable step, tooth root injury</td><td>Lumps, swelling, rare blood-vessel blockage</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Duration</td><td>Long-term unless removed</td><td>Long-term once healed</td><td>Temporary, usually months</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

