<h2 id="which-suits-whom">Which option suits which situation?</h2>
<h3>An implant may suit you if</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Your chin is mildly to moderately set back but of normal height</li>
  <li>Your bite is normal or has already been corrected</li>
  <li>You prefer a shorter operation and accept that an implant is a foreign material</li>
</ul>
<h3>A genioplasty may suit you if</h3>
<ul>
  <li>The chin needs a larger movement, or needs to be shortened, lengthened or straightened</li>
  <li>You have had problems with an implant before, or would rather avoid one</li>
  <li>You are already having jaw surgery and the chin needs adjusting as part of the same plan</li>
</ul>
<h3>Jaw surgery may be the better answer if</h3>
<p>If your lower jaw as a whole sits back and your upper front teeth stick out well in front of the lower ones, a chin procedure alone can make the profile look better while leaving the bite problem untouched. In that situation, <a href="/orthognathic-surgery">orthognathic surgery</a> to bring the lower jaw forward, with or without a genioplasty, may address both appearance and function. Our article on <a href="/blogs/can-jaw-surgery-improve-facial-appearance/">whether jaw surgery can improve facial appearance</a> explains how this is decided.</p>
<p>Fillers are reasonable for people who want a small, temporary change, or who would like to see how a more prominent chin looks before considering surgery.</p>

<h2 id="recovery-and-risks">Recovery and risks</h2>
<p>After either operation, swelling and tightness are usual, peaking around the second or third day. A supportive chin dressing or tape is often used for the first few days. Most people return to desk work in about one to two weeks after an implant and two to three weeks after a genioplasty, but this varies. Bruising fades over a couple of weeks, and the final shape is usually judged after several months, once all the swelling has settled.</p>
<p>With an incision inside the mouth, you will need a soft diet for a short period and careful mouth rinsing after meals. Smoking and tobacco, including gutka, slow healing and increase the risk of infection and should be stopped well before surgery.</p>
<p>Possible risks include:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Numbness or tingling</strong> of the lower lip and chin, usually temporary but occasionally lasting</li>
  <li><strong>Infection</strong>, which with an implant may mean the implant has to be removed</li>
  <li><strong>Asymmetry or an unsatisfactory shape</strong>, sometimes needing revision</li>
  <li><strong>Implant displacement</strong> or pressure on the underlying bone</li>
  <li><strong>A palpable step</strong> at the lower border of the jaw after genioplasty, or problems with the plate</li>
  <li><strong>Injury to tooth roots</strong>, which careful planning aims to avoid</li>
  <li><strong>Sagging of the chin soft tissue</strong> if the chin muscle does not heal well after an incision inside the lip</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="when-to-see-a-specialist">When to see a specialist</h2>
<p>It is worth an assessment if you feel your chin is small, set back or uneven, if you are considering rhinoplasty and want to understand how the chin affects your profile, or if you have a bite problem and are unsure whether chin surgery alone would help. Facial growth should be complete before chin surgery, so it is generally offered to adults.</p>
<p>At a consultation in Gachibowli, we examine your face and bite, discuss what you would like to change, and explain honestly which options are realistic, including non-surgical ones and the option of no treatment. If surgery is right for you, it is carried out at NABH-accredited partner hospitals in Hyderabad. You can read more about the procedures we offer on our <a href="/chin-augmentation-surgery">chin augmentation surgery</a> page.</p>
